course: dictionary
lesson: 7008
title: Parasites and their hosts
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A parasite is a living thing that lives on or inside another living thing and takes its food from it. That other living thing is called its host, and the host is often harmed. Fleas, lice, ticks and tapeworms are all parasites.
explain: The word can also describe a person who lives off other people and gives nothing back. This meaning is unkind, so use it carefully.
explain: Look at the clues in this sentence: «An adult gives the flea and worm treatment the vet suggests, to keep pets and the family free of parasites.» The treatment is there to get rid of parasites, so parasites are something a family wants to be free of.
fact: A parasite gets food or shelter from its host, and the host is harmed.
fact: In science, a parasite and its host are a key pair.
fact: «Parasitic worms» are worms that live as parasites.
fact: «Rafflesia arnoldii» from Sumatra and Borneo has the biggest single flower, about 1 metre across, and it lives as a parasite inside a vine.
exercise: What is a parasite? | A plant that makes its own food from sunlight | *A living thing that lives on or inside another living thing and takes its food from it | A living thing that never eats anything
exercise: «Rafflesia has no leaves, stems or roots of its own and lives inside a vine as a parasite.» In this sentence, what is the host? | The sunlight | Rafflesia | *The vine | The flower
exercise: «Cleaner wrasse pick parasites and dead skin off big fish, and the big fish does not eat its cleaner, so both win.» What does the cleaner wrasse get? | Nothing at all | *Food from the parasites and dead skin | A ride on a buffalo | Its own flower
test: Why does the vet suggest flea and worm treatment? | To turn pets into hosts | To give pets a new home | To make pets grow bigger | *To keep pets and the family free of parasites
test: A person who lives off other people and gives nothing back is called what, in an unkind way? | A cleaner | A tapeworm | A host | *A parasite
exam: What is the difference between a parasite and its host? | They both always gain something | Neither one is ever harmed | *The parasite gets food or shelter, and the host is harmed | The host gets food and the parasite is harmed
